<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#gorani-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gorani language</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gorani is a Northwestern Iranian language spoken in parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> on both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> border, closely related to Zazaki. From the 16th to the 19th century it served as the literary language of the Ardalan court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanandaj">Sanandaj</a> and of many Kurdish poets, before Sorani largely replaced it. It is also the sacred language of the Yarsani faith.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gorani-language.jpg" alt="Gorani language" width="320" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gorani.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Gorani, also called Gurani, and known by the name of its main dialect, Hawrami, is a Northwestern Iranian language, or a group of similar dialects, spoken in small pockets in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Its name in Kurdish means song. Its linguistic homeland is believed to lie somewhere around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, and it is suggested that its speakers migrated together from that region to their present settlements at an unknown early date.</p><p>Classification is disputed. Ethnologue places it with Zaza in a Zaza-Gorani subgroup, though significant differences between the two have been found, and Glottolog places it in an Adharic subgroup. Broader classifications include it among the Kurdish languages. The Hawrami linguist Mihemed Emîn Hewramanî argued that Hawrami is a dialect within the unified tree of Kurdish. The great majority of its speakers consider their language to be Kurdish, though some emphasise an identity distinct from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Gorani comprises dialects including Bajelani, Hawrami, Sarli and Shabaki, and most lack gender and case, except Hawrami. Hawrami, regarded as the most archaic, is spoken mainly in the Hawraman region, on both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> border, by around 23,000 people, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> classed it as definitely endangered in 2010. Bajelani has about 59,000 speakers, mostly around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, and Sarli fewer than 20,000.</p><p>Gorani had an estimated 180,000 speakers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and 120,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in 2007. Many speakers in Iran also speak Sorani, Persian and Southern Kurdish, and most in Iraq also speak Sorani, while some speak Mesopotamian Arabic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Under the independent rulers of Ardalan, with their capital latterly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanandaj">Sanandaj</a>, Gorani became the vehicle of a considerable corpus of poetry. Hawrami was the traditional literary language and koiné of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> in the historical Ardalan region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a>, but was later supplanted by Central Kurdish and Southern Kurdish. Gorani remains the first language of the scriptures of the Ahl-e Haqq, or Yarsani, sect. The names of forty classical poets are known, but most of their lives are undated. Perhaps the earliest is Mele Perîşan, who is reported to have lived about 1356–1431.</p><p>Gorani verse consists almost entirely of stanzas of two rhyming half-verses of ten syllables each. Poets include Khana Qubadi, who wrote Shirin and Khosrow in 1740. Long masnavis, mostly translated from Persian literature, include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun">Layla and Majnun</a> and Khosrow and Shirin, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> are kept in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gorani language” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court patronised poetry in <b>Gorani</b>, the literary Kurdish of the region, and in the 19th century Mastoureh Ardalan wrote a history of the dynasty, one of the first by a Kurdish woman.</blockquote><a href="/state/ardalan">Ardalan</a></li></ul></section>
